Pooled blocks of 16 MiB at most instead of 64 MiB: on a guest whose device memory is wired guest RAM a pool's
blocks double up to the cap and stay wired whole (NativeMetal 45c8294's bridge patch 0021, lost at the move to 555b13d).

--- a/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/memory.rs
+++ b/crates/metal2vulkan-runtime/src/memory.rs
@@ -168,8 +168,13 @@
 pub(crate) mod making_parts;
 mod ready;
 
-/// The size of a pooled block, at most the device's `maxMemoryAllocationSize`.
-pub(super) const BLOCK_BYTES: u64 = 64 << 20;
+/// The size of a pooled block, at most the device's `maxMemoryAllocationSize`. 16 MiB: where device
+/// memory is the system's own and wired as a block is made (a guest whose GPU imports its pages),
+/// a pool's blocks double up to this ([`FIRST_BLOCK_BYTES`]) and a partly filled or kept empty one
+/// of 64 MiB stays wired whole: a macOS guest's WindowServer held 623 MiB in blocks of up to 80 MiB
+/// and its icon agent 267 MiB (two blocks of 64 MiB and one of 68) where a new one that drew the
+/// same icons held 121 MiB in blocks of 4 to 24 MiB (2026-11-16).
+pub(super) const BLOCK_BYTES: u64 = 16 << 20;
 
 /// What a pooled block of images holds past [`BLOCK_BYTES`], as a fraction of it: a sixteenth
 /// ([`Memory::pooled_bytes`]). A driver's image takes more than its texels: RADV on a Radeon 780M
